# Expectation record for 07_variadics/003_struct_and_pointer_args.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 003_struct_and_pointer_args
area               = 07_variadics
description        = Aggregates and pointers passed through the variadic path
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Aggregates are not subject to the default argument promotions, so va_arg names exactly the
type supplied.  Because the pointer retrieval type is pointer-to-const-int and int and
const int are not compatible types, every pointer argument is written with an explicit cast
to the retrieval type, so no argument of the wrong type is ever retrieved.  Only the
pointee value is printed, never a pointer.  No list is read past its argument count and
each is ended exactly once.  <stdarg.h> is included because a variadic function cannot be
written without it and it is in both compilers' freestanding sets; printf is still
hand-declared, because bcc ships no stdio.h.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
